{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FKmuBycHsHLwxJT2vZ2UYd","type":"tool_result","content":"title:\trefactor: backend split behind a Backend enum (#15)\nstate:\tDRAFT\nauthor:\tuser (Amogh Akshintala)\nlabels:\t\nassignees:\t\nreviewers:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nnumber:\t29\nurl:\thttps://github.com/user/delegate/pull/29\nadditions:\t1080\ndeletions:\t824\nauto-merge:\tdisabled\n--\nFixes #15\n\n- `Backend` enum (cursor only), with one `match` per method: `from_name`, `bin`, `argv`, `spawn`. The job runner's test seam is the `Runner` trait.\n- `BackendResult` is normalized: text, session id, usage, reported cost, error, duration. Cost comes from the backend when it reports one, otherwise from the model table.\n- The argv, capability flags, binary resolution and doctor probes moved into `backends/cursor`. `capability.rs` and `cursor_bin.rs` are deleted.\n- A table-driven parser test runs on every `tests/fixtures/cursor/*.stdout`.\n- No cursor-agent flag or binary name appears outside `src/backends/`.\n\nImplemented by grok-4.7-xhigh via `scripts/record.sh`.\n\nCo-authored with Claude Code.\nauthor:\tuser\nassociation:\towner\nedited:\tfalse\nstatus:\tnone\n--\n## Preflight rulings: orchestrator ruling (owner asleep; reversible)\n\n1. **Interface:** an enum `Backend { Cursor }` (pi and claude variants arrive in #16 and #17), with `match` in each method. No `dyn` trait: three closed variants don't need one. Its methods are `bin`, `argv(model, capability, cwd, session)`, `parse` (stream to a normalized result: text, session id, usage, optional cost, error), `resume_args`, and doctor probes. The CLI `doctor` command stays where #11 put it and calls the probes.\n2. **Encapsulation:** `build_argv`, the capability-to-flag mapping and the cursor binary resolution move into `backends/cursor`. Delete the non-spec capability modes if #13 left any.\n3. **Parsers:** `stream.rs` stays at the crate root, so the #17 Claude parser can reuse it. STATUS-line parsing stays outside the backend, in `output.rs`.\n4. **Fixture tests:** one test for each `tests/fixtures/cursor/*.stdout`. Keep the existing inline tests.\n5. **Failure cases:**\n   - A bad model (empty stdout, exit 1) is an error built from the exit code and stderr.\n   - Lines that don't parse are skipped.\n   - A clean exit with no `result` line is ERROR.\n   - CANCELLED is set by the supervisor, never the parser.\n6. **Behaviour:** unchanged. Existing tests may change their imports only.\n--\ntitle:\tSpec: delegate, a multi-backend CLI replacing the cursor-delegate MCP server\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tuser (Amogh Akshintala)\nlabels:\tready-for-agent\ncomments:\t1\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nissue-type:\t\nparent:\t\nsub-issues:\t\nsub-issues-completed:\t\nblocked-by:\t\nblocking:\t\nnumber:\t4\n--\n## Problem Statement\n\ncursor-delegate lets a host agent hand work to Cursor's models, but only Cursor's, and only from hosts that load its MCP server. The models I actually use are spread across three harnesses: Cursor (Composer, Grok), pi (ChatGPT-subscription GPT models, opencode-go models) and Claude Code (Claude on the Max plan). None of them can reach the others' models.\n\nThe tool has also grown more surface than it uses. Across 413 real `cursor_run` calls, every one ran in the background, `plan` capability was never used, `allowPartialCommit` and `idleMs` were never set, `cursor_wait_any` ran twice, and several parameters (`verifyCommands`, `promptPreamble`, `requireNonClaude`, the `allowUnsandboxed` second signal) only add prompt text or guard against cases that never happen. Eight MCP tools and fourteen run parameters cost schema tokens in every session and make the skill long.\n\nRunning as an MCP server adds its own costs: one resident process per host session, job state that dies with the session, a write lock that cannot see writes from another session, and per-harness registration (Claude plugin, Codex plugin manifest).\n\n## Solution\n\nReplace the `cursor-delegate` MCP server with a CLI called `delegate`, plus one skill. Any harness with a shell can call it. Every configured model is reachable from every harness, routed to one of three backends: cursor-agent, pi, or Claude Code.\n\n`delegate run` starts a job and returns its id immediately. Each job runs under its own detached supervisor that writes a per-job status file. Callers wait with `delegate watch` run in the background and read results from the status file. A finished job can be continued with `delegate resume`. The run surface shrinks to the parameters that real use needs.\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As an orchestrating agent, I want to start a delegated job with one shell command, so that I can delegate from any harness that has a shell.\n2. As an orchestrating agent, I want `delegate run` to print the job id and return at once, so that my turn is never blocked on a model.\n3. As an orchestrating agent, I want to pass the prompt on stdin or from a file, so that long briefs need no shell quoting.\n4. As an orchestrating agent, I want to choose a model by id, so that the backend follows from the model without a separate flag.\n5. As an orchestrating agent, I want a default model when I omit one, so that cheap bulk work needs no extra flag.\n6. As an orchestrating agent, I want an unknown model id rejected with the list of valid ids, so that a typo fails fast.\n7. As an orchestrating agent, I want `read-only` and `read-write` capabilities, so that I can say whether a delegate may change files.\n8. As an orchestrating agent, I want `read-only` to be the default, so that a forgotten flag never grants writes.\n9. As an orchestrating agent, I want a pi model with `read-only` rejected, so that I never believe a pi run is read-only when pi cannot enforce it.\n10. As an orchestrating agent, I want to set the delegate's working directory, so that it works in a worktree or scratch directory I prepared.\n11. As an orchestrating agent, I want the working directory to default to where I ran the command, so that the common case needs no flag.\n12. As an orchestrating agent, I want a second read-write job in the same directory refused with BUSY, so that two delegates never interleave edits.\n13. As an orchestrating agent, I want that lock to hold across separate host sessions, so that two Claude sessions cannot collide in one tree.\n14. As an orchestrating agent, I want to give a gate command the tool runs after the agent, so that I get ground-truth verification instead of the delegate's claim.\n15. As an orchestrating agent, I want a failing gate to downgrade DONE to DONE_WITH_CONCERNS with the gate's output, so that I see what failed.\n16. As an orchestrating agent, I want the result to include the git change-set the tool computed, so that I know what actually changed.\n17. As an orchestrating agent, I want a concern when commits landed but the tree is still dirty, so that half-committed work is visible.\n18. As an orchestrating agent, I want to widen the tool-call idle window for one job, so that a long build or benchmark is not killed as hung.\n19. As an orchestrating agent, I want a job that goes silent past its idle window killed and reported, so that hung jobs end on their own.\n20. As an orchestrating agent, I want to wait on one or many jobs with `delegate watch` in a background shell, so that my turn can end and I get one notification when they finish.\n21. As an orchestrating agent, I want `delegate watch` to print every job's final record, so that one command both waits and delivers results.\n22. As an orchestrating agent, I want `delegate watch` to stop at a timeout, so that a missing or never-written record cannot hang it forever.\n23. As an orchestrating agent, I want to read a job's progress by reading its status file, so that polling needs no tool.\n24. As an orchestrating agent, I want the status file to carry a heartbeat while running, so that I can tell a dead supervisor from a slow job.\n25. As an orchestrating agent, I want the final status file to hold the full result (status, text, usage, cost, change-set, gate result, session), so that the file is the whole answer.\n26. As an orchestrating agent, I want the delegate's final STATUS line to set the result status, including NEEDS_CONTEXT, so that I know when it needs me.\n27. As an orchestrating agent, I want to resume any finished job with a follow-up prompt, so that I can answer a question or ask for fixes without re-briefing.\n28. As an orchestrating agent, I want resume to reuse the job's model, directory, capability and gate, so that I cannot forget a setting on the follow-up.\n29. As an orchestrating agent, I want to override capability, gate or model on resume, so that a read-only reviewer can go on to fix what it found.\n30. As an orchestrating agent, I want a model override that crosses backends rejected, so that I never try to resume a cursor session in pi.\n31. As an orchestrating agent, I want resume to work after the host session or machine sleep, until reboot, so that follow-ups do not depend on a live server.\n32. As an orchestrating agent, I want resume to return a new job id, so that each run has its own record.\n33. As an orchestrating agent, I want to cancel a job by id, so that I can stop a runaway delegate and the commands it started.\n34. As an orchestrating agent, I want `delegate models` to list ids, labels, backends and prices, so that I can pick a model without reading config.\n35. As a user, I want to see per-run cost, so that I know what delegation would cost at list prices.\n36. As a user, I want cursor runs priced from the model table and pi and claude runs to report their own cost, so that no backend's pricing is reimplemented.\n37. As a user, I want prices for every model in the model table, so that `delegate models` shows them all.\n38. As a user, I want `delegate doctor` to check each backend's binary, version and login, so that setup problems show before a run fails.\n39. As a user, I want doctor to check each configured model is available on its backend, so that renamed or retired models (Grok 4.6 to 4.7) show as warnings.\n40. As a user, I want pi and Claude Code to run with my full configuration (extensions, plugins, skills, context files), so that delegates behave as they do when I run them.\n41. As a user, I want cursor read-write runs unsandboxed, so that delegates can clone, push and open PRs.\n42. As a user, I want Claude Code read-write runs in auto permission mode, so that its classifier still screens tool calls.\n43. As a user, I want Claude Code read-only runs in plan permission mode, so that they cannot edit.\n44. As a user, I want the model list trimmed to models I use, so that the menu is short.\n45. As a user, I want Composer 2.5, Grok 4.7 high and xhigh from cursor; muse-spark-1.3-contributor, glm-5.3-flash, deepseek-v4.1-flash from opencode-go via pi; gpt-6-astra, gpt-6-luna, gpt-6.1-sol from openai-codex via pi; and Claude Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Fable 5.1 via Claude Code.\n46. As a user, I want the project renamed from cursor-delegate to delegate everywhere (binary, config directory, environment variable, status-file directory, skill, repo), so that the name matches what it does.\n47. As a user, I want my existing host profile moved to the new config location by setup, so that my settings survive the rename.\n48. As a user, I want setup to build the binary and put `delegate` on my PATH, so that every harness can call it.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"daed6cef-788d-4be7-9254-e50472bc43cc","uuid":"6ae6202d-1b13-4807-bd7e-edc1794fd725","timestamp":"2026-10-01T11:41:46.310Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"title:\trefactor: backend split behind a Backend enum (#15)\nstate:\tDRAFT\nauthor:\tuser (Amogh Akshintala)\nlabels:\t\nassignees:\t\nreviewers:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nnumber:\t29\nurl:\thttps://github.com/user/delegate/pull/29\nadditions:\t1080\ndeletions:\t824\nauto-merge:\tdisabled\n--\nFixes #15\n\n- `Backend` enum (cursor only), with one `match` per method: `from_name`, `bin`, `argv`, `spawn`. The job runner's test seam is the `Runner` trait.\n- `BackendResult` is normalized: text, session id, usage, reported cost, error, duration. Cost comes from the backend when it reports one, otherwise from the model table.\n- The argv, capability flags, binary resolution and doctor probes moved into `backends/cursor`. `capability.rs` and `cursor_bin.rs` are deleted.\n- A table-driven parser test runs on every `tests/fixtures/cursor/*.stdout`.\n- No cursor-agent flag or binary name appears outside `src/backends/`.\n\nImplemented by grok-4.7-xhigh via `scripts/record.sh`.\n\nCo-authored with Claude Code.\nauthor:\tuser\nassociation:\towner\nedited:\tfalse\nstatus:\tnone\n--\n## Preflight rulings: orchestrator ruling (owner asleep; reversible)\n\n1. **Interface:** an enum `Backend { Cursor }` (pi and claude variants arrive in #16 and #17), with `match` in each method. No `dyn` trait: three closed variants don't need one. Its methods are `bin`, `argv(model, capability, cwd, session)`, `parse` (stream to a normalized result: text, session id, usage, optional cost, error), `resume_args`, and doctor probes. The CLI `doctor` command stays where #11 put it and calls the probes.\n2. **Encapsulation:** `build_argv`, the capability-to-flag mapping and the cursor binary resolution move into `backends/cursor`. Delete the non-spec capability modes if #13 left any.\n3. **Parsers:** `stream.rs` stays at the crate root, so the #17 Claude parser can reuse it. STATUS-line parsing stays outside the backend, in `output.rs`.\n4. **Fixture tests:** one test for each `tests/fixtures/cursor/*.stdout`. Keep the existing inline tests.\n5. **Failure cases:**\n   - A bad model (empty stdout, exit 1) is an error built from the exit code and stderr.\n   - Lines that don't parse are skipped.\n   - A clean exit with no `result` line is ERROR.\n   - CANCELLED is set by the supervisor, never the parser.\n6. **Behaviour:** unchanged. Existing tests may change their imports only.\n--\ntitle:\tSpec: delegate, a multi-backend CLI replacing the cursor-delegate MCP server\nstate:\tOPEN\nauthor:\tuser (Amogh Akshintala)\nlabels:\tready-for-agent\ncomments:\t1\nassignees:\t\nprojects:\t\nmilestone:\t\nissue-type:\t\nparent:\t\nsub-issues:\t\nsub-issues-completed:\t\nblocked-by:\t\nblocking:\t\nnumber:\t4\n--\n## Problem Statement\n\ncursor-delegate lets a host agent hand work to Cursor's models, but only Cursor's, and only from hosts that load its MCP server. The models I actually use are spread across three harnesses: Cursor (Composer, Grok), pi (ChatGPT-subscription GPT models, opencode-go models) and Claude Code (Claude on the Max plan). None of them can reach the others' models.\n\nThe tool has also grown more surface than it uses. Across 413 real `cursor_run` calls, every one ran in the background, `plan` capability was never used, `allowPartialCommit` and `idleMs` were never set, `cursor_wait_any` ran twice, and several parameters (`verifyCommands`, `promptPreamble`, `requireNonClaude`, the `allowUnsandboxed` second signal) only add prompt text or guard against cases that never happen. Eight MCP tools and fourteen run parameters cost schema tokens in every session and make the skill long.\n\nRunning as an MCP server adds its own costs: one resident process per host session, job state that dies with the session, a write lock that cannot see writes from another session, and per-harness registration (Claude plugin, Codex plugin manifest).\n\n## Solution\n\nReplace the `cursor-delegate` MCP server with a CLI called `delegate`, plus one skill. Any harness with a shell can call it. Every configured model is reachable from every harness, routed to one of three backends: cursor-agent, pi, or Claude Code.\n\n`delegate run` starts a job and returns its id immediately. Each job runs under its own detached supervisor that writes a per-job status file. Callers wait with `delegate watch` run in the background and read results from the status file. A finished job can be continued with `delegate resume`. The run surface shrinks to the parameters that real use needs.\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As an orchestrating agent, I want to start a delegated job with one shell command, so that I can delegate from any harness that has a shell.\n2. As an orchestrating agent, I want `delegate run` to print the job id and return at once, so that my turn is never blocked on a model.\n3. As an orchestrating agent, I want to pass the prompt on stdin or from a file, so that long briefs need no shell quoting.\n4. As an orchestrating agent, I want to choose a model by id, so that the backend follows from the model without a separate flag.\n5. As an orchestrating agent, I want a default model when I omit one, so that cheap bulk work needs no extra flag.\n6. As an orchestrating agent, I want an unknown model id rejected with the list of valid ids, so that a typo fails fast.\n7. As an orchestrating agent, I want `read-only` and `read-write` capabilities, so that I can say whether a delegate may change files.\n8. As an orchestrating agent, I want `read-only` to be the default, so that a forgotten flag never grants writes.\n9. As an orchestrating agent, I want a pi model with `read-only` rejected, so that I never believe a pi run is read-only when pi cannot enforce it.\n10. As an orchestrating agent, I want to set the delegate's working directory, so that it works in a worktree or scratch directory I prepared.\n11. As an orchestrating agent, I want the working directory to default to where I ran the command, so that the common case needs no flag.\n12. As an orchestrating agent, I want a second read-write job in the same directory refused with BUSY, so that two delegates never interleave edits.\n13. As an orchestrating agent, I want that lock to hold across separate host sessions, so that two Claude sessions cannot collide in one tree.\n14. As an orchestrating agent, I want to give a gate command the tool runs after the agent, so that I get ground-truth verification instead of the delegate's claim.\n15. As an orchestrating agent, I want a failing gate to downgrade DONE to DONE_WITH_CONCERNS with the gate's output, so that I see what failed.\n16. As an orchestrating agent, I want the result to include the git change-set the tool computed, so that I know what actually changed.\n17. As an orchestrating agent, I want a concern when commits landed but the tree is still dirty, so that half-committed work is visible.\n18. As an orchestrating agent, I want to widen the tool-call idle window for one job, so that a long build or benchmark is not killed as hung.\n19. As an orchestrating agent, I want a job that goes silent past its idle window killed and reported, so that hung jobs end on their own.\n20. As an orchestrating agent, I want to wait on one or many jobs with `delegate watch` in a background shell, so that my turn can end and I get one notification when they finish.\n21. As an orchestrating agent, I want `delegate watch` to print every job's final record, so that one command both waits and delivers results.\n22. As an orchestrating agent, I want `delegate watch` to stop at a timeout, so that a missing or never-written record cannot hang it forever.\n23. As an orchestrating agent, I want to read a job's progress by reading its status file, so that polling needs no tool.\n24. As an orchestrating agent, I want the status file to carry a heartbeat while running, so that I can tell a dead supervisor from a slow job.\n25. As an orchestrating agent, I want the final status file to hold the full result (status, text, usage, cost, change-set, gate result, session), so that the file is the whole answer.\n26. As an orchestrating agent, I want the delegate's final STATUS line to set the result status, including NEEDS_CONTEXT, so that I know when it needs me.\n27. As an orchestrating agent, I want to resume any finished job with a follow-up prompt, so that I can answer a question or ask for fixes without re-briefing.\n28. As an orchestrating agent, I want resume to reuse the job's model, directory, capability and gate, so that I cannot forget a setting on the follow-up.\n29. As an orchestrating agent, I want to override capability, gate or model on resume, so that a read-only reviewer can go on to fix what it found.\n30. As an orchestrating agent, I want a model override that crosses backends rejected, so that I never try to resume a cursor session in pi.\n31. As an orchestrating agent, I want resume to work after the host session or machine sleep, until reboot, so that follow-ups do not depend on a live server.\n32. As an orchestrating agent, I want resume to return a new job id, so that each run has its own record.\n33. As an orchestrating agent, I want to cancel a job by id, so that I can stop a runaway delegate and the commands it started.\n34. As an orchestrating agent, I want `delegate models` to list ids, labels, backends and prices, so that I can pick a model without reading config.\n35. As a user, I want to see per-run cost, so that I know what delegation would cost at list prices.\n36. As a user, I want cursor runs priced from the model table and pi and claude runs to report their own cost, so that no backend's pricing is reimplemented.\n37. As a user, I want prices for every model in the model table, so that `delegate models` shows them all.\n38. As a user, I want `delegate doctor` to check each backend's binary, version and login, so that setup problems show before a run fails.\n39. As a user, I want doctor to check each configured model is available on its backend, so that renamed or retired models (Grok 4.6 to 4.7) show as warnings.\n40. As a user, I want pi and Claude Code to run with my full configuration (extensions, plugins, skills, context files), so that delegates behave as they do when I run them.\n41. As a user, I want cursor read-write runs unsandboxed, so that delegates can clone, push and open PRs.\n42. As a user, I want Claude Code read-write runs in auto permission mode, so that its classifier still screens tool calls.\n43. As a user, I want Claude Code read-only runs in plan permission mode, so that they cannot edit.\n44. As a user, I want the model list trimmed to models I use, so that the menu is short.\n45. As a user, I want Composer 2.5, Grok 4.7 high and xhigh from cursor; muse-spark-1.3-contributor, glm-5.3-flash, deepseek-v4.1-flash from opencode-go via pi; gpt-6-astra, gpt-6-luna, gpt-6.1-sol from openai-codex via pi; and Claude Opus 5.5, Sonnet 5.5, Fable 5.1 via Claude Code.\n46. As a user, I want the project renamed from cursor-delegate to delegate everywhere (binary, config directory, environment variable, status-file directory, skill, repo), so that the name matches what it does.\n47. As a user, I want my existing host profile moved to the new config location by setup, so that my settings survive the rename.\n48. As a user, I want setup to build the binary and put `delegate` on my PATH, so that every harness can call it.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PU4rHWPZYWfhyuJ7FvtL96","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (76.2KB). Full output saved to: /Users/user/.claude/projects/-Users-user-work-delegate-15-backend-split/daed6cef-788d-4be7-9254-e50472bc43cc/tool-results/b8v5ifc3e.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\ndiff --git a/src/backends/cursor.rs b/src/backends/cursor.rs\ndeleted file mode 100644\nindex 263403a..0000000\n--- a/src/backends/cursor.rs\n+++ /dev/null\n@@ -1,238 +0,0 @@\n-//! The cursor-agent backend: spawn the child, parse stream-json (NDJSON) from stdout,\n-//! report progress/liveness events, and return a BackendResult when it exits.\n-\n-use super::types::{Backend, BackendResult, Event, EventFn, ProgressSnapshotRaw, Spawned};\n-use crate::stream::{StreamState, init_stream_state, parse_line};\n-use crate::types::{JobSpec, RawCursorJson};\n-use std::io::Read;\n-use std::os::unix::process::CommandExt;\n-use std::process::{Command, Stdio};\n-use std::sync::Arc;\n-use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, Ordering};\n-\n-/// Only a 2 KB tail of stderr is ever reported; keep a bounded window of it.\n-const STDERR_KEEP: usize = 64 * 1024;\n-\n-pub struct CursorAdapter;\n-\n-pub fn make_cursor_adapter() -> CursorAdapter {\n-    CursorAdapter\n-}\n-\n-pub fn build_argv(\n-    model: &str,\n-    cap_flags: &[String],\n-    session: Option<&str>,\n-    prompt: &str,\n-) -> Vec<String> {\n-    let mut argv = vec![\n-        \"--print\".into(),\n-        \"--output-format\".into(),\n-        \"stream-json\".into(),\n-        \"--trust\".into(),\n-        \"--approve-mcps\".into(),\n-        \"--model\".into(),\n-        model.to_string(),\n-    ];\n-    argv.extend(cap_flags.iter().cloned());\n-    if let Some(s) = session {\n-        argv.push(\"--resume\".into());\n-        argv.push(s.to_string());\n-    }\n-    argv.push(\"--\".into());\n-    argv.push(prompt.to_string());\n-    argv\n-}\n-\n-impl Backend for CursorA